        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 48.9" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="571" ed="#liebermann"/>Nec testes audientes uel uidentes habendi sunt de eo quod non<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="42" target="#Hn_48-9.lnote.oe.42"/> fuerit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="43" target="#Hn_48-9.lnote.oe.43"/>, sed quod accusatus fuisse contendit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="44" target="#Hn_48-9.lnote.oe.44"/>.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 571 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page); Liebermann’s Erklärung (Gesetze III p. 323) has ‘Zengen’ (a turned u; read ‘Zeugen’); transcribed as printed</note>
